speechtext:
At the present time the United Grain Growers are carrying on a pool system of cattle shipment. I would suggest that the government guarantee the men who put their animals into the chilled meat trade the same price they would obtain if they shipped them alive to Liverpool. Nothing more than that as an experiment. On looking over the figures of the Harris Abattoir Company, I find that when they carried on the business steadily the beef was gaining in favour with the English people, but the shipments were allowed to become irregular. In spite of the fact that the company lost a lot of money, I think the committee will be interested to hear what Mr. McLean said about the experiment. These are his words;
The losses became so heavy that we were forced to discontinue the shipments. However, an analysis of the results gives some ground for optimism. The loss on the shipments was equivalent to about one penny farthing per pound. We were convinced that the chilled beef which we shipped was worth on the British market two pence per pound and probably three pence per .pound more than we actually received for it. And we were further convinced that had conditions been such as to permit continuous shipments this Canadian chilled .beef would very soon have commanded a price relatively much higher than that received for our experimental shipments.

He also stated, and it is absolutely true, that the British butcher was not selling this as chilled meat at all, but as English meat. It may be necessary for this country to do the same as Argentine and New Zealand,- establish a shop or two over there to sell our product and so let the people of England really know the quality of Canadian beef. I do not think objections should be raised by any member about somebody else paying for the experiment for the benefit of the farmer. As I pointed out to-night, farmers have contributed to the revenue $4,000,000- it does not matter what head the money goes under, it has gone into the treasury-and I think it would be only right and proper, especially to-day when those engaged in the cattle trade are finding themselvs in a deplorable condition, for the government to take action along this line. Surely it is worth something to the country that our farmers should not go out of the cattle business. But, as I said before, unless some relief can be found, unless rates can be brought down -and I very much doubt if they can be-the only relief is in the development of this chilled beef business.
